reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey:

Step 0: seek #tab-account (initial state)
Then ArrowRight -> expects focus moves to #tab-notifications and aria-selected becomes true on notifications.
But observed: after pressing ArrowRight, focus remained on body (the whole page?), actually focus was still on body? Let's look at observed step details.

Observed failed steps:
Action: {"key":"ArrowRight","kind":"press"}
Before: bbox shows button #tab-account (active).
After: state shows focus on body (selector "body") with outline etc. So focus didn't move to #tab-notifications.

Also assertions: expected attribute aria-selected on #tab-notifications true (passed) but focus assertion failed (focus not on #tab-notifications).

Thus issue: ArrowRight keydown handler prevented default and stopped propagation, then computed next active element via getNextActiveElement, filtered children where !isDisabled(element). It then calls Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show().

But why didn't focus move? Possibly because the nextActiveElement returned is null or not a tab? Or maybe the show() method didn't set focus on the tab because after activation, the tab element loses tabindex? Actually in _activate, after adding active class, they call this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) to activate panel, then in complete callback they remove tabindex and set aria-selected true. Wait: In _activate, they do:

element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_ACTIVE)

this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) // Search and activate/show the proper section

const complete = () => {
  if (element.getAttribute('role') !== 'tab') {
    element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)
    return
  }

  element.removeAttribute('tabindex')
  element.setAttribute('aria-selected', true)
  this._toggleDropDown(element, true)
  EventHandler.trigger(element, EVENT_SHOWN, { relatedTarget: relatedElem })
}

So after activation, they remove tabindex (so tab becomes not focusable?) Actually they remove tabindex attribute entirely, making it not focusable via keyboard? But they also set aria-selected true. However, for a tab to be focusable, it needs to be either tabindex="0" or not have tabindex negative? Usually tablist expects tabs to be focusable (tabindex="0" for active, -1 for inactive). In the code, they remove tabindex for active tab, which would make it not focusable (unless it's naturally focusable like a button). Since the tab is a button element, it is naturally focusable (button). Removing tabindex shouldn't affect focusability because button is focusable by default. However, they also call element.blur() in _deactivate for the previously active tab. That's fine.

But why focus didn't move to the next tab? Perhaps because getNextActiveElement returned null due to filtering disabled? Let's examine the markup: all tabs are buttons, not disabled. The _getChildren returns SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM which includes .nav-link, .list-group-item, [role="tab"] plus data-toggle. Should find the three tab buttons.

In _keydown, they do:

const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren().filter(element => !isDisabled(element)), event.target, isNext, true)

They filter out disabled elements (none). Then getNextActiveElement(list, activeElement, shouldGetNext, isCycleAllowed). The list is array of tab buttons. activeElement is event.target (the current tab). isNext true for ArrowRight. isCycleAllowed true (they passed true as fourth arg). So getNextActiveElement should return the next element in list, wrapping if needed.

Thus nextActiveElement should be the notifications tab.

Then they call Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show().

Now, show() method: It checks if this._elemIsActive(innerElem) (if already active) return. It then finds active sibling via _getActiveElem() (which returns currently active tab). Then fires hide/show events, then calls this._deactivate(active, innerElem) and this._activate(innerElem, active).

Thus show should activate the next tab and deactivate current.

But why focus didn't move? Possibly because after show(), the focus remains on the previous tab? Actually they didn't explicitly focus the new tab. In Bootstrap tabs, keyboard navigation should move focus to the new tab. Usually they rely on the fact that the tab is a button and when you press arrow key, you prevent default and then programmatically focus the next tab. However, in this code they call show() on the next tab instance, which activates it but does not focus it. The focus remains on the previous tab? Actually after keydown, they prevented default and stopped propagation, so the natural focus movement due to arrow key on a radiogroup is prevented. They need to manually focus the next tab. The show() method does not focus the tab; it just updates ARIA and classes. So focus stays on the previously active tab (which after deactivation gets tabindex="-1"? Wait, in _deactivate they set tabindex="-1" on the old tab (if role !== tab? Actually they do:

if (element.getAttribute('role') !== 'tab') {
  element.classList.remove(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)
  return
}

element.setAttribute('aria-selected', false)
element.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1')
this._toggleDropDown(element, false)
...

So after deactivation, the old tab gets tabindex="-1", making it not focusable. Meanwhile the new tab, in _activate, they remove tabindex (if role===tab) i.e., they remove tabindex attribute entirely. For a button, removing tabindex leaves it focusable (default). So after activation, the new tab should be focusable (button). However, focus hasn't moved; maybe because the browser doesn't automatically move focus when you remove tabindex from one element and add to another unless you call focus().

Thus we need to modify the code to focus the newly activated tab after activation. According to ARIA authoring practices, when moving focus with arrow keys, you should set focus to the new tab.

Thus we need to add focus() call to the newly activated tab element after activation.

Where to add? In _activate complete callback after setting aria-selected true, we can also focus the element.

Alternatively, in show() after activating, we could focus the new tab.

Let's look at the _activate method: after adding active class, they call this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) to activate panel, then complete callback runs after transition (if fade). In complete, they set aria-selected true and remove tabindex. We can add element.focus() there.

But note: element is the tab button. However, if the tab is a button, calling focus() will focus it. That should work.

Edge case: if the tab is not focusable (maybe custom element), but still we should attempt.

Thus edit: In tab.js, inside _activate method's complete function, after setting aria-selected true, add element.focus();

But careful: The complete callback is queued via this._queueCallback(complete, element, element.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_FADE)). If there is no fade, queueCallback executes callback synchronously? Actually executeAfterTransition: if isAnimated false, it executes callback immediately. So focus will be called either after transition or immediately.

